Seminar: Plato on being, difference, falsehood, and knowledge (@greek-seminar-plato-being: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-seminars, #source @greek-source-sophist, #source @greek-source-plato-critical, #source @greek-source-cordero, #source @greek-source-theaetetus-modern, #philosophy @greek-seminar-study, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-seminar-study) { r_34WRXN1SRY8SZVRKRV7X3X9QWA
**Preparation:** [Parmenides](../../presocratics/Parmenides.lingua#r_7HVRAJY7AF3178NEH1N8BF9NPV), [Theaetetus](../../plato/Theaetetus.lingua#r_3T0C5M7DVRZDE4ENZ3Z0V0AV2N), Republic VI–VII, and the textual and literature dossiers.

**Backbone:** read [Sophist](../../sources/Sophist-Jowett.lingua#r_4B67CBBBRAQS5SCS5M5K8RGXXR) as a complete dialogue; read the connected arguments of Republic VI–VII and the relevant surviving poem of Parmenides. This combination follows an actual [graduate bibliography benchmark](../../sources/Academic-benchmarks.lingua#r_7YDPSHERTRH41D7YR4P93PWNEP); the investigation and assessment below are Lince's authored route.

Use the [critical Greek text](../../sources/Plato-critical-editions.lingua#r_28N1ADM4R3DAG5K2898WPKRXCK) and [Cordero's translated commentary](../../sources/Sophist-Cordero.lingua#r_5MPQY66DGXV4RRE8GCWCCKJ952) or a justified scholarly equivalent. Return to [Theaetetus](../../sources/Theaetetus-Levett-Burnyeat.lingua#r_615423T7F74G6W17SFHWMSMDV9) when the problem depends on knowledge, judgment, or an account.

**Choose a precise dispute:** the relation between Parmenides' difficulty and the Stranger's account; the roles of the greatest kinds; the distinction between being different and an absolute absence of being; or how a statement can be false. Read the argument about predication and combination in the whole dialogue, including its investigation of the sophist.

Reconstruct competing interpretations of the uses of “is”, participation, identity, and difference. A modern formalisation can test an inference, but it must explain which features of the Greek and dialogue it preserves. Distinguish that reconstruction from a claim that Plato already held the later formal theory.

**Required work:** a Greek commentary on a decisive exchange, an argument map across the complete Sophist, an account of two competing readings, and a defended thesis about what the text establishes. Explain how the earlier Parmenidean or epistemological comparison changes the argument.

**Further depth:** Plato's Parmenides and Philebus, using an identified edition and a question about criticism of forms, unity and plurality, or dialectical method.

**Review:** [Common seminar standard](../Sustained-study.lingua#r_7WN2AF9HW3X3BNQSTF5CKFJ22Q). **Return:** [Seminar families](Seminars.lingua#r_5FTSQDJTR924S9Z0G9EDBQFR1Y).
